DDEESSCCRRIIPPTTIIOONN
     The DDEECCLLAARREE__MMOODDUULLEE() macro declares a generic kernel module.  It is used
     to register the module with the system, using the SSYYSSIINNIITT() macro.
     DDEECCLLAARREE__MMOODDUULLEE() is usually used within other macros, such as
     DRIVER_MODULE(9), DEV_MODULE(9) and SYSCALL_MODULE(9).  Of course, it can
     also be called directly, for example in order to implement dynamic
     sysctls.

     A module declared with DDEECCLLAARREE__MMOODDUULLEE__TTIIEEDD() will load only if the run‐
     ning kernel version (as specified by __FreeBSD_version) is identical to
     that on which it was built.  This declaration should be used by modules
     which depend on interfaces beyond the stable kernel KBI (such as ABI emu‐
     lators or hypervisors that rely on internal kernel structures).
     DDEECCLLAARREE__MMOODDUULLEE() will behave like DDEECCLLAARREE__MMOODDUULLEE__TTIIEEDD() when compiled
     with modules built with the kernel.  This allows locks and other synchro‐
     nization primitives to be inlined safely.

     The arguments are:

     _n_a_m_e    The module name, which will be used in the SSYYSSIINNIITT() call to
             identify the module.

     _d_a_t_a    A _m_o_d_u_l_e_d_a_t_a___t structure, which contains two main items, the of‐
             ficial name of the module name, which will be used in the
             _m_o_d_u_l_e___t structure and a pointer to the event handler function of
             type _m_o_d_e_v_e_n_t_h_a_n_d___t.

     _s_u_b     An argument directed to the SSYYSSIINNIITT() macro.  Valid values for
             this are contained in the _s_y_s_i_n_i_t___s_u_b___i_d enumeration (see
             <_s_y_s_/_k_e_r_n_e_l_._h>) and specify the type of system startup inter‐
             faces.  The DRIVER_MODULE(9) macro uses a value of SI_SUB_DRIVERS
             here for example, since these modules contain a driver for a de‐
             vice.  For kernel modules that are loaded at runtime, a value of
             SI_SUB_EXEC is common.

     _o_r_d_e_r   An argument for SSYYSSIINNIITT().  It represents the KLDs order of ini‐
             tialization within the subsystem.  Valid values are defined in
             the _s_y_s_i_n_i_t___e_l_e_m___o_r_d_e_r enumeration (<_s_y_s_/_k_e_r_n_e_l_._h>).

